Subject: [PATCH] fix(virtual-net): rearm loopback writes after receive

The loopback socket suppresses repeated writable polls with a one-shot flag.
If an event loop consumed that event before it had queued a response write,
the socket remained writable but no later writable poll could complete. This
starved delayed libuv socket.end(data) writes until the peer disconnected.

Rearm the local transmit half after a successful receive. This supports the
request-response transition without turning every idle writable socket into a
level-triggered busy loop. The CPU-throttled cross-guest browser proof covers
the regression.
---
 src/tcp_pair.rs | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/tcp_pair.rs b/src/tcp_pair.rs
--- a/src/tcp_pair.rs
+++ b/src/tcp_pair.rs
@@ -169,6 +169,17 @@ impl SocketBuffer {
         state.state
     }
 
+    fn rearm_write_ready(&self) {
+        let mut state = self.state.lock().unwrap();
+        state.halt_immediate_poll_write = false;
+        if !state.buffer.is_full() {
+            if let Some(handler) = state.pull_handler.as_mut() {
+                handler.push_interest(InterestType::Writable);
+            }
+            state.wakers.drain(..).for_each(|w| w.wake());
+        }
+    }
+
     pub fn try_send(
         &self,
         data: &[u8],
@@ -465,6 +476,10 @@ impl VirtualConnectedSocket for TcpSocketHalf {
         buf: &mut [std::mem::MaybeUninit<u8>],
         peek: bool,
     ) -> crate::Result<usize> {
-        self.rx.try_read(buf, peek, None)
+        let result = self.rx.try_read(buf, peek, None);
+        if result.is_ok() {
+            self.tx.rearm_write_ready();
+        }
+        result
     }
 }
